%% SECTION Integrated Scenarios
@@
You take over a hybrid project midway. The previous project manager left no handover notes. The team uses sprints for software while a vendor builds hardware under a fixed-price contract. What should you do first?
A) Review the plan, contracts, backlog, risk register and issue log, and meet the team and key stakeholders
B) Re-plan the whole project from scratch so that the new plan reflects your own approach and assumptions
C) Renegotiate the vendor contract as time and materials to gain flexibility for the remaining hardware work
D) Ask the team to move to a fully predictive approach so that the whole project is managed in one way
= A
? A new PM first builds understanding from existing artifacts and people before changing anything.

@@
A product owner wants to add a regulatory reporting feature mid-release. The release date is fixed by law, and the team's capacity is full. What is the best approach?
A) Re-prioritise the backlog with the product owner so the mandatory feature fits, deferring lower-value items
B) Add the feature to the release and ask the team to work overtime until the release date is reached
C) Reject the feature, since the release scope was agreed and cannot change once the release has started
D) Ask the authorities to move the legal deadline so that the team can deliver the feature in the next release
= A
? In adaptive delivery with fixed time, scope is traded based on value and obligations; mandatory compliance items take priority.

@@
During execution of a predictive construction project, the client asks for higher-grade windows. The change would add $80,000 and two weeks. The contingency reserve is $50,000 and is reserved for identified risks. What should the project manager do?
A) Document the change with its impact and funding needs and submit it through change control
B) Use the contingency reserve to pay for the windows, since the reserve exists for unplanned costs
C) Approve the change directly, since the client is the one requesting and funding the upgrade
D) Refuse the request, since the scope baseline cannot be modified once construction has started
= A
? A scope change is not a risk event, so contingency reserve should not fund it. The change goes through integrated change control with its impact on cost and schedule.

@@
Halfway through an agile project, velocity drops by 30% over two sprints. The team says they are spending a lot of time on unplanned production support. What should the project manager or scrum master do?
A) Make the support work visible and agree with the team and product owner how to handle it
B) Tell the team to stop doing production support so that development velocity can recover
C) Report the team's lower productivity to management so that it can be addressed formally
D) Lengthen the sprints from two to three weeks so that more points are completed per sprint
= A
? Transparency and joint problem-solving address the root cause of the drop while respecting real operational needs.

@@
A key stakeholder requests weekly detailed reports, but the communications plan specifies monthly dashboards. Producing weekly reports would take one analyst two days per week. What should the project manager do?
A) Explore the stakeholder's underlying information needs and agree a proportionate solution
B) Decline the request, since the communications plan has been approved and is the baseline
C) Produce the weekly reports, since stakeholder satisfaction justifies the analyst's effort
D) Ask the sponsor to tell the stakeholder that the monthly dashboard is all that is available
= A
? Understanding needs and negotiating a proportionate solution balances stakeholder satisfaction with project effort.

@@
A vendor informs the project that a critical component will be four weeks late because of a supplier fire. The contract includes a force majeure clause. What should the project manager do first?
A) Assess the schedule impact, review the contract terms and explore options with the vendor
B) Terminate the contract for cause, since the vendor has failed to meet the agreed date
C) Apply the liquidated damages clause, since the delay is clearly the vendor's responsibility
D) Wait for the component quietly, since informing stakeholders would only create concern
= A
? Impact assessment and contract review come first; force majeure may excuse the delay, so penalties may not apply.

@@
The team is about to deploy a new system when a tester finds a security vulnerability. The sponsor wants to go live on schedule. What should the project manager do?
A) Inform the sponsor of the vulnerability and its impact and recommend options per security policy
B) Go live as planned and fix the vulnerability in the next release, without informing anyone about it
C) Postpone go-live indefinitely until every possible vulnerability has been eliminated
D) Ask the tester to reclassify the finding as minor so that the go-live can proceed
= A
? Transparency, compliance with security policy and accountable leadership require informing the decision-maker with options.

@@
A team member uses a public generative AI service to write code and pastes in proprietary source code from the client. What should the project manager do?
A) Stop the practice, assess the exposure with security and legal teams and reinforce AI policies
B) Encourage the practice, since using AI to write code improves the team's productivity and morale
C) Take no action, since the generated code worked and the client has not complained
D) Ban all software development tools on the project to avoid any further incidents
= A
? Confidentiality and responsible AI use require immediate containment, assessment and reinforcement of policy.

@@
At a phase gate, the business case shows that expected benefits have fallen below the investment cost because of a market change. What is the most appropriate recommendation?
A) Present the updated business case and options, including termination, to the steering committee
B) Keep the updated figures confidential, so that the team remains motivated until the end of the project
C) Continue the project, since a large part of the budget has already been spent
D) Expand the scope to increase the benefits, without changing the approved business case
= A
? Focusing on value means decision-makers should know when the business case no longer holds; sunk costs are irrelevant.

@@
In a matrix organisation, a functional manager reassigns a key developer to another project without warning. What should the project manager do first?
A) Discuss the impact with the functional manager and look for a solution together
B) Escalate the matter straight to the CEO so that the decision is reversed quickly
C) Tell the developer to keep working on your project regardless of the reassignment
D) Stop the affected work and wait until a new developer is assigned by the organisation
= A
? Direct negotiation with the resource manager is the first step; escalation follows if the conflict cannot be resolved.

@@
A distributed team spanning four time zones complains that decisions are made in meetings some members cannot attend. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree inclusive working agreements with the team, such as rotating meeting times and recorded decisions
B) Keep the meetings at the times that suit the project manager, since the PM coordinates the whole team
C) Ask members in inconvenient time zones to leave the team and replace them with local people
D) Stop holding meetings and rely on written status updates sent by email once a week
= A
? Inclusive working agreements ensure every member can participate in decisions.

@@
The organisation adopts a new sustainability policy requiring carbon reporting for all projects. Your project is in execution. What should you do?
A) Assess the new requirements, plan data collection and reporting, and process changes through governance
B) Ignore the policy, since the project started before it was introduced and has an approved plan
C) Pause the project until the end of the year, when the policy's reporting cycle will begin
D) Let the sustainability team estimate the project's emissions alone, without involving the project team
= A
? New organisational requirements are assessed and incorporated through governance, consistent with the sustainability principle.

@@
Two senior stakeholders disagree on the project's primary objective: one wants speed to market and the other wants maximum features. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a session using the business case and strategy, escalating to the sponsor if needed
B) Choose speed to market, since it is usually the better choice in competitive markets
C) Try to satisfy both by adding all of the features and also accelerating the delivery schedule
D) Wait for the stakeholders to resolve the disagreement between themselves
= A
? Aligning expectations requires facilitation grounded in the business case; the sponsor decides if they cannot agree.

@@
A risk owner reports that the trigger for an identified supplier risk has occurred, but the planned response is no longer feasible because the alternative supplier closed. What should happen?
A) Use the fallback plan if one exists, or a workaround, then update the risk register
B) Take no action, since the risk was identified in advance and the response has already been planned
C) Close the risk in the register, since its trigger has now occurred and passed
D) Transfer the risk to the team, which is now responsible for finding a supplier
= A
? When the primary response fails, a fallback plan or a workaround is used, followed by updates to risk documentation.

@@
A new team member joins a high-performing agile team. Shortly afterwards, the team shows signs of conflict and slower decisions. What is the most likely explanation and action?
A) The team may have regressed to an earlier stage; support onboarding and revisit team agreements
B) The new member is not competent enough and should be replaced as soon as possible
C) The team should stop holding retrospectives until the new member is fully integrated into the team
D) The conflict is unrelated to the new member, so no particular action is needed
= A
? Membership changes often cause teams to revisit earlier stages; onboarding and refreshing agreements help them return to performing.

@@
The CPI of a project is 0.75 and the SPI is 1.05. The sponsor asks for the main concern. What is the best answer?
A) Costs: the project is well over budget while slightly ahead of schedule
B) Schedule: the project is behind and needs to be fast-tracked urgently
C) Neither: both indices are within the normal range for a project at this stage
D) Quality: a CPI below 1 shows that the defect rate is above the agreed target
= A
? CPI below 1 means overspending; SPI above 1 means slightly ahead. The cost overrun is the priority.

@@
An agile team discovers that the definition of done does not include accessibility testing, although the product must meet legal accessibility standards. What should be done?
A) Add accessibility checks to the definition of done and put the gaps in completed work on the backlog
B) Wait until users complain about accessibility, and then decide whether any changes are worth making
C) Ask users to report accessibility problems after release so they can be fixed in support
D) Remove accessibility from the requirements, since it was not in the definition of done
= A
? Compliance requirements should be embedded in the DoD; existing gaps are added to the backlog and prioritised.

@@
During closure, the operations team refuses to accept the system because support documentation is missing. What should the project manager do?
A) Complete the missing transition deliverables required by the acceptance criteria before closing
B) Close the project anyway, since the system itself has been delivered and tested successfully by the team
C) Ask the sponsor to instruct operations to accept the system without the documentation
D) Hand the documentation task to the operations team, since they will use the system
= A
? Transition readiness is part of the deliverables; closure requires formal acceptance by the receiving organisation.

@@
A project in a government agency must comply with strict procurement rules, but the team wants to bring in a specialist consultant within days. What is the most appropriate action?
A) Work with procurement to use an approved, compliant route such as an existing framework agreement
B) Engage the consultant informally and reimburse the fees through the team's expense claims
C) Skip the procurement rules this once, since the business need is urgent and the request is well justified
D) Ask the consultant to start working for free until the formal contract has been signed
= A
? Compliance with procurement rules is mandatory; approved mechanisms may provide speed without breaking rules.

@@
A sponsor asks the project manager to commit to a delivery date before any estimates exist. What is the best response?
A) Explain the uncertainty, give a range and agree when a more reliable estimate will be available
B) Give the date that the sponsor would like, since meeting the sponsor's expectations is the top priority
C) Refuse to discuss any dates until the requirements and design have been fully completed
D) Propose an aggressive date to motivate the team and adjust it later if needed
= A
? Honest communication of uncertainty with progressive elaboration supports good decisions and trust.
